.TH "ELSERENO.YAML" "5" "2026\-04\-19" "ElSereno configuration" "File formats"
.SH NAME
\f[B]elsereno.yaml\f[R]: configuration file for \f[I]elsereno\f[R](1)
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]\(ti/.config/elsereno/elsereno.yaml\f[R],
\f[B]./elsereno.yaml\f[R], or any path passed via the global
\f[B]\-\-config\f[R] flag.
.SH DESCRIPTION
ElSereno reads its configuration from the first of:
.IP "1." 3
\f[B]\-\-config\f[R] \f[I]path\f[R]
.IP "2." 3
\f[B]$ELSERENO_CONFIG\f[R]
.IP "3." 3
\f[B]$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/elsereno/elsereno.yaml\f[R]
.IP "4." 3
\f[B]\(ti/.config/elsereno/elsereno.yaml\f[R]
.IP "5." 3
\f[B]\(ti/.elsereno/elsereno.yaml\f[R]
.IP "6." 3
\f[B]./elsereno.yaml\f[R]
.PP
Unknown keys are rejected at parse time with the error
\f[B]ErrUnknownConfigField\f[R] (see \f[B]elsereno\-security\f[R](7)).
.SH FIELDS
Durations are written as text (\f[B]30s\f[R], \f[B]2m0s\f[R]); a bare
number, a negative duration or an unknown key stops the command with
exit 78.
Until 2026\-10\-17 a file with any duration or list did not load at all
(PITF\-096).
The defaults are what \f[B]elsereno config show\f[R] prints.
.PP
Read by the binary:
.PP
\f[B]scanner.max_concurrent_targets\f[R] (default \f[B]100\f[R])
\f[B]scanner.max_concurrent_per_host\f[R] (default \f[B]1\f[R]) :
Concurrency caps of \f[B]scan\f[R] and of the dashboard scans of
\f[B]serve\f[R]; \f[B]max_concurrent_per_host\f[R] also caps
\f[B]discover\f[R].
A \f[B]\-\-profile\f[R] other than \f[B]normal\f[R], and the
\f[B]\-\-max\-concurrent\f[R] and \f[B]\-\-max\-conns\-per\-host\f[R]
flags, override them for that run.
.PP
\f[B]database.max_conns\f[R] (default \f[B]10\f[R])
\f[B]database.tls_required\f[R] in {\f[B]auto\f[R], \f[B]always\f[R],
\f[B]disable\f[R]} (default \f[B]auto\f[R]) : Postgres pool size and
TLS; see \f[B]elsereno\-security\f[R](7).
The connection itself comes from \f[B]DATABASE_URL\f[R].
.PP
\f[B]web.read_header_timeout\f[R] (default \f[B]5s\f[R])
\f[B]web.read_timeout\f[R] (default \f[B]30s\f[R])
\f[B]web.write_timeout\f[R] (default \f[B]30s\f[R])
\f[B]web.idle_timeout\f[R] (default \f[B]2m0s\f[R]) : HTTP server
timeouts of \f[B]serve\f[R]; zero means the built\-in default.
.PP
\f[B]web.rate_limit_per_min_ip\f[R] (default \f[B]100\f[R])
\f[B]web.rate_limit_per_min_token\f[R] (default \f[B]300\f[R]) : Request
caps of \f[B]serve\f[R]: a token bucket per client IP (loopback exempt;
IPv6 by /64) and per Bearer token or, without one, \f[B]X\-Operator\f[R]
name (in any auth mode; loopback included).
Over either cap: \f[B]429\f[R] with \f[B]Retry\-After: 60\f[R];
\f[B]/healthz\f[R] and \f[B]/readyz\f[R] are never limited; \f[B]0\f[R]
turns that cap off (so does a negative value).
\f[B]X\-Forwarded\-For\f[R] is ignored, so behind a same\-host reverse
proxy every client is exempt loopback: rate limit in that proxy.
.TP
\f[B]auth.oidc.issuer\f[R], \f[B]auth.oidc.audience\f[R], \f[B]auth.oidc.jwks_url\f[R]
OIDC for \f[B]serve\f[R]; required on a non\-loopback address.
.PP
Accepted but not read by anything yet (setting them changes nothing):
\f[B]retention.findings_days\f[R], \f[B]retention.evidence_days\f[R],
\f[B]retention.runs_days\f[R], \f[B]evidence.max_payload_bytes\f[R],
\f[B]log.level\f[R], \f[B]log.output\f[R],
\f[B]shutdown.drain_timeout\f[R], \f[B]doctor.ntp_server\f[R],
\f[B]web.token_ttl_days\f[R], \f[B]web.token_generation_cache_ttl\f[R],
\f[B]web.max_body_bytes\f[R], \f[B]readyz.audit_tail_entries\f[R] (only
the stub \f[B]audit verify\f[R]), \f[B]exec.allowed_paths\f[R] (it does
not limit which binaries run), \f[B]outbox.max_attempts\f[R], and
\f[B]auth.oidc.clock_skew\f[R] (fixed at 60s in the code).
